---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
const title = "CAGR and XIRR — Which Return Number to Use";
const description =
  "CAGR assumes one lump sum in and one value out. XIRR handles irregular cash " +
  "flows. Using the wrong one misstates your return, often badly.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Investing / CAGR and XIRR">
  <h1>CAGR and XIRR</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Both express a return as an annual rate. They differ in what they assume about
    when money went in &mdash; and using the wrong one produces a number that can be
    badly wrong rather than slightly wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>CAGR</h2>
  <div class="formula">CAGR = (End value / Start value)^(1 / years) &minus; 1</div>
  <p class="lede">
    The constant annual rate that would take a starting value to an ending value.
    It uses only the first and last figures, so an investment that fell 60% and
    recovered has the same CAGR as one that rose steadily to the same point. The
    experience of holding them was not remotely the same.
  </p>
  <p>Use it when: one amount in at the start, one value at the end. Nothing else.</p>

  <h2>XIRR</h2>
  <p>
    When money goes in and out on irregular dates &mdash; an SIP, top-ups, partial
    withdrawals &mdash; CAGR has no valid input. XIRR is the rate at which every cash
    flow, discounted back to the first date, sums to zero.
  </p>
  <div class="formula">&Sigma;  CF&#8342; / (1 + r)^(d&#8342; / 365)  =  0        solved for r</div>
  <p class="lede">
    There is no closed form; it is solved numerically. The
    <a href="/tools/xirr-calculator/">XIRR calculator</a> does it the same way a
    spreadsheet does.
  </p>

  <h2>The error this prevents</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose you invest &#8377;10,000 a month for five years &mdash; &#8377;6 lakh total &mdash; and
    it is worth &#8377;8 lakh at the end.
  </p>
  <div class="formula">Wrong:  treat 6 lakh as invested for 5 years
        (8/6)^(1/5) &minus; 1 = 5.9%

Right:  XIRR over the actual monthly dates
        roughly 11-12%</div>
  <p class="lede">
    The wrong method understates the return by roughly half, because the average
    rupee was invested for about two and a half years, not five. The last
    instalment was invested for one month.
  </p>

  <h2>Which to use</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def"><div class="h"><code>lumpsum_investment</code><span class="meta">CAGR</span></div>
      <p>One purchase, one current value.</p></div>
    <div class="def"><div class="h"><code>sip_or_any_recurring</code><span class="meta">XIRR</span></div>
      <p>Money going in at different times. This is most people, most of the time.</p></div>
    <div class="def"><div class="h"><code>comparing_funds</code><span class="meta">CAGR, carefully</span></div>
      <p>Published fund returns are point-to-point CAGR. That is the fund's
        performance, not yours &mdash; yours depends on when you actually invested.</p></div>
    <div class="def"><div class="h"><code>your_own_portfolio</code><span class="meta">XIRR</span></div>
      <p>Almost always, because almost nobody invests once and never again.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>What neither tells you</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Both compress an entire history into one number, and say nothing about the
    path. Two portfolios with identical XIRR can have had completely different
    drawdowns, and the one that fell 45% along the way was a different thing to
    own. Return without risk is half a description.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>
  <p class="lede">Next: <a href="/learn/compounding/">compounding</a> &middot;
    <a href="/tools/xirr-calculator/">XIRR calculator</a> &middot;
    <a href="/learn/inflation/">inflation</a></p>
</Base>
